[38;5;231m⏺[39m Got it — login plus signup. Next fork, and this one has consequences worth laying out before you pick.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how the shared validation file gets loaded. Right now the repo is split: [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m as a plain
  script tag (browser globals, no modules), while [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m/[38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m use CommonJS [38;5;153mrequire[39m. A shared validation
  module has to pick one, and that choice decides whether the same file can be unit-tested in Node without a build step.

  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153mvalidation.js[39m uses [38;5;153mexport[39m; [38;5;153mindex.html[39m becomes [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m. Browsers load it natively, and
    Node imports the exact same file for tests. Costs one line in the HTML and leaves [38;5;153msrc/[39m's CommonJS as the odd one
    out. No build tooling.
  - [1mCommonJS[0m — matches [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m exactly, tests trivially in Node, but browsers can't load [38;5;153mrequire[39m without a bundler.
    That means adding a build step, which this repo doesn't have, or a dual-format shim.
  - [1mBrowser global[0m — [38;5;153mvalidation.js[39m sets [38;5;153mwindow.Validation[39m, added as a second [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag. Matches today's [38;5;153mapp.js[39m style
    with zero tooling, but the file isn't importable in Node, so testing needs jsdom or a workaround.

  Cheap to change later: the HTML script tag. Expensive: if we go global-only and then want real unit tests, the module
  gets rewritten and every call site with it.

  [1mMy recommendation is ES modules[0m — it's the only option that gets browser loading and Node-testable validation logic
  without introducing a bundler this project currently has no reason to own.
